{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 20, :comments ["<b>Justice, justice shall you pursue.</b> Go after a fine court. <i>That you may live and possess the land</i> — the appointment of qualified judges is worthy to give life to Israel and to settle them on their soil; this is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Deuteronomy 16:20) from Sifrei (Sifrei 144). The reason for the doubling is to say that the judges must judge the people with just judgment, and also you must pursue justice constantly — that you go from your place to the place of great sages, after Rabban Yohanan ben Zakkai to Yavneh, after Rabbi to Beit She'arim. And Rabbi Abraham Ibn Ezra said (Ibn Ezra on Deuteronomy 16:20): צֶדֶק צֶדֶק twice — that you pursue justice whether you profit by it or lose by it, or time after time for reinforcement. But in the midrash of Rabbi Nehunya ben HaKanah they interpret in it a secret. They said: צֶדֶק — this is the attribute of His justice of the world, as it is written, צֶדֶק צֶדֶק תִּרְדֹּף, and after it is written, לְמַעַן תִּחְיֶה וְיָרַשְׁתָּ אֶת הָאָרֶץ. If you judge yourself you shall live; if not, He will judge you and fulfill it against your will. And what is צֶדֶק צֶדֶק — two times? As it is written (II Samuel 22:13), מִנֹּגַהּ נֶגְדּוֹ עָרְפָלִים — the first צֶדֶק is צֶדֶק מַמָּשׁ, actual justice; this is the Shekhinah, as it is written (Isaiah 1:21), צֶדֶק יָלִין בָּהּ. And what is the second צֶדֶק? That which terrifies the righteous. And he explains there: צֶדֶק is like one wearing יְשׁוּעָה upon his head, and רֹאשׁ means nothing but אֱמֶת, as it is written (Psalms 119:160), רֹאשׁ דְּבָרְךָ אֱמֶת, and אֱמֶת means nothing but שָׁלוֹם, as it is written (II Kings 20:19), הֲלֹא אִם שָׁלוֹם וֶאֱמֶת יִהְיֶה בְיָמַי וגו'. If so, Scripture says: you shall judge in your court and pursue צֶדֶק צֶדֶק and attain them, לְמַעַן תִּחְיֶה — for the sake of the World to Come — in the second צֶדֶק, which is supreme צֶדֶק, and it is the great light hidden away for the righteous for the future to come, and it is His might of the Holy One, blessed be He. וְיָרַשְׁתָּ אֶת הָאָרֶץ — in the first צֶדֶק, which is the Land of Israel."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 16, :notes {0 "The citations of II Samuel 22:13 and II Kings 20:19 in the midrashic passage follow the numbering of the Hebrew Bible; II Samuel 22:13 in some editions aligns with II Samuel 22:12 in others. The phrase הקב\"ה has been rendered as 'the Holy One, blessed be He.'"}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T02:50:52.180422246Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}